<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">French protectorate of Tunisia</h1><p class="st-meta">1881 CE – 1956 CE · capital Tunis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tunisia under French rule from 1881, where the bey reigned but a French resident-general governed, until independence in 1956.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Using border raids as a pretext, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beylik-of-tunis">Beylik of Tunis</a> in 1881 and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iii-as-sadiq">Muhammad as-Sadiq Bey</a> to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bardo">Treaty of Bardo</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marsa-convention">La Marsa Convention</a> of 1883 placed internal administration under French control, with the bey retained as nominal sovereign.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A French resident-general held real power, with French officials running finance, public works and justice for Europeans. French and Italian settlers acquired farmland for olives, vines and grain, and phosphate mining expanded. Tunisian nationalism grew through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/destour">Destour</a> party, founded in 1920, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habib-bourguiba">Habib Bourguiba's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-destour">Neo-Destour</a> from 1934. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moncef-bey">Moncef Bey</a> was deposed by the French in 1943.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After armed resistance and negotiations, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> granted internal autonomy in 1955 and full independence in March 1956. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-amin-bey">Lamine Bey</a> became king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tunisia">Kingdom of Tunisia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habib-bourguiba">Bourguiba</a> as prime minister.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-protectorate-of-tunisia.jpg" alt="French protectorate of Tunisia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file: · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Beylik_of_Tunis_(1831%E2%80%931881)_and_Tunisia_(1881%E2%80%931959).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1881 CE – 1956 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tunis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad as-Sadiq Bey, Moncef Bey, Muhammad al-Amin Bey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>French Tunisia, Protectorate of Tunisia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=french-protectorate-of-tunisia" aria-label="French protectorate of Tunisia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4935 851 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French protectorate of Tunisia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5255 1107L5255 1107M5255 1107L5054 728M5255 1107L5255 1107"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5255 1107h0M5054 728h0M5255 1107h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5255 1107h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5255 1107h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tunis. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=french-protectorate-of-tunisia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
